  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through next Tuesday)...\n\nActive weather today and Severe Weather Threat...Significant \nMUCAPE values of 1500-2000 J/kg are progged for our eastern \ncounties today along a weak surface convergent area with PWATs \nover an inch. As monsoonal impulses track over this area we expect\nto see strong to severe convection fire-up and move into the \nDakotas by evening. Local winds to 65 mph, very heavy downpours, \nfrequent lightning are the primary threats, but cannot rule out \nsome hail as well with stronger cells. The rest of the CWA will \nsee scattered convection as well late day, some of which may \nproduce local wind gusts to 50-55 mph. CAMs indicate active \nconvection with showers and isolated thunderstorms continuing \novernight and then lingering over our east Thursday morning. \n\nBig Picture...A series of weak shortwaves embedded in SW flow \naloft, combined with elevated monsoon moisture, will result in a \nbusy period of showers and thunderstorms through Thursday. \nCoverage of storms may begin to decrease by Friday with little if \nany convection expected for the weekend. Hot temps will continue \nthrough the work week before peaking Saturday where models progs\nare indicating a 700 mb thermal ridge reaching up to 19C shifting\nover our region. This should push temps well over 100F for much of\nour CWA Saturday. Daily records for Aug 1st will be in jeopardy, \nand would not be surprised to see some locations reach 110F (smoke\npermitting) for the 2nd time this summer. At this time, the best \nchance of seeing temps over 105F Saturday are along and near the \nYellowstone River Valley from Billings to Miles City (45-60%).\n\nBy Sunday, ensembles confidently suggest a breakdown of the upper\nridge as a short wave tracks over the MT/Canada border. This will\nlead to breezy/windy conditions by Sunday, followed by cooler \ntemps to start the next work week. Think highs in the lower 80s \nfor Monday/Tuesday with little chance of precip. \n\nFire Weather...mixed winds will be breezy at times, but mid level\nflow on the order of 10-20kts does not support strong winds. \nFurthermore, a weak cold front looks to arrive after midnight  \nSaturday night with a secondary push Sunday night. All told, \nSaturday will see elevated fire wx concerns, but frontal timing \nseems late enough to prevent it from being a more problematic day.\nBreezy post frontal conditions Sunday could be an issue as \nhumidity will still drop into the teens. Will need to monitor \nfrontal passage timing for the weekend.\n\nWildfire Smoke...smoke will be sporadic over the next few days,\nbut models indicate surface based smoke will not be to bad over\nour CWA. The frontal passages this weekend could be followed by \nincreased surface smoke per the subsidence and developing upslope \nwinds...but confidence is low at this time. BT",
